`;
---

<LessonLayout title="EX2 — Technical Support Assistant: RAG Design Exercise" num="EX2">

  <LessonHeader
    num="EX2"
    title="Technical Support Assistant — RAG Design Exercise"
    subtitle="All 10 cookbook phases applied to a RAG-powered support agent: hybrid search, citations as state, agentic vs pipeline retrieval, hallucination prevention, and retrieval evaluation."
  />

  <LessonCallout variant="tip" title="How to use this exercise">
    <p>
      This exercise applies the Agent Design Cookbook (CB) to a system where
      <strong>retrieval is the core capability</strong>, not a supporting tool.
      Work through each phase in order — the design decisions build on each other.
      Read the staff-level design review at the end before writing any code:
      those questions reveal the RAG-specific failure modes that phases alone don't surface.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Connects to:</strong> L17 (RAG Pipeline) for retrieval implementation ·
      CB (Agent Design Cookbook) for the phase framework ·
      EX1 (Sales Order Agent) for contrast — that exercise uses RAG as one tool among many;
      here it is the entire value proposition.
    </p>
  </LessonCallout>

  <!-- ── Scenario ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 class="lesson-heading">Scenario — HelixDB Cloud Technical Support</h2>
  <p>
    HelixDB is a time-series database SaaS. Developers integrate via an SDK and REST API.
    The support team fields hundreds of technical questions per day through a chat widget
    embedded in the developer portal. Most questions fall into four categories:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>How-to</strong> — "How do I configure retention policies?"</li>
    <li><strong>Error investigation</strong> — "I'm getting ERR_SCHEMA_CONFLICT on every write"</li>
    <li><strong>Version changes</strong> — "What broke between v2.3 and v2.5?"</li>
    <li><strong>Escalation</strong> — data loss, billing questions, SLA violations</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The knowledge base consists of: versioned product documentation (getting-started guide,
    API reference, configuration guide, performance tuning guide, migration guides),
    a known-issues database (bugs, workarounds, severity, affected versions),
    and a changelog. All three are updated by the engineering team on each release.
  </p>

  <!-- ── Worked example ────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 class="lesson-heading">Worked Example — Applying the 10 Phases</h2>

  <!-- Phase 01 -->
  <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 01 — Do you need an agent?</h3>
  <div class="lesson-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Decision question</th><th>Answer for HelixDB support</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Fixed, known steps with no branching?</td>
          <td>No — each question requires a different retrieval query, different tools, and different formatting. An FAQ that always retrieves first is a pipeline, not this.</td>
          <td>—</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Output is just text, no side effects?</td>
          <td>No — the agent may need to create a support ticket, look up an existing ticket, or fetch a changelog. Side effects are conditional on what retrieval returns.</td>
          <td>—</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Dynamic decision-making: which tool to call, in which order, based on what retrieval returns?</td>
          <td>Yes — a documentation question may pivot to a known-issue search if docs return nothing relevant. A known-issue search may spawn a ticket if no workaround exists. The path through the tools is not fixed.</td>
          <td><strong>Agent justified.</strong></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <LessonCallout variant="tip" title="RAG pipeline vs agentic RAG — the split happens here">
    <p>
      A simple document Q&amp;A bot that always retrieves first, then generates, is a
      <strong>RAG pipeline</strong> — a chain, not an agent. No autonomy needed.
    </p>
    <p>
      This system needs an agent because the retrieval decision is conditional:
      which knowledge source to search (docs vs known issues vs changelog), whether to search
      at all (version-query uses <code>get_changelog</code>, not a vector search), and whether
      to create a ticket after retrieval (conditional on what came back). That conditionality
      is exactly what agentic RAG adds over a pipeline.
    </p>
  </LessonCallout>

  <!-- Phase 02 -->
  <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 02 — Define the goal and the problem</h3>
  <div class="phase-def">
    <div class="pd-row"><span class="pd-label">Goal</span><span class="pd-val">Resolve developer technical questions about HelixDB Cloud without human involvement for issues that are documented — while creating a traceable ticket for everything that isn't.</span></div>
    <div class="pd-row"><span class="pd-label">Success metric</span><span class="pd-val">Self-serve resolution rate ≥ 70% · P95 response &lt; 4 s · 100% of answers cite a verifiable source · Escalation rate ≤ 30% · Zero hallucinated answers that pass citation check</span></div>
    <div class="pd-row"><span class="pd-label">Input</span><span class="pd-val">Developer question (chat) + conversation history + authenticated user context (product version, support plan, open tickets)</span></div>
    <div class="pd-row"><span class="pd-label">Output</span><span class="pd-val">Cited technical answer OR support ticket created + ticket ID returned OR escalation to human queue</span></div>
    <div class="pd-row"><span class="pd-label">"Done" means</span><span class="pd-val">Question answered with at least one verifiable citation — OR ticket created — OR developer acknowledged escalation. The agent never exits without one of these three outcomes.</span></div>
    <div class="pd-row"><span class="pd-label">Hard constraints</span><span class="pd-val">Every factual claim must be cited · Never answer from training knowledge if not in retrieved context · Never reveal another customer's ticket · Response &lt; 4 s P95</span></div>
    <div class="pd-row"><span class="pd-label">Acceptable failure</span><span class="pd-val">"I don't have documentation on this" followed by create_support_ticket(). A known gap in the KB is acceptable. An uncited answer that sounds authoritative is not.</span></div>
  </div>

  <!-- Phase 03 -->
  <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 03 — Choose your autonomy level: autonomy per action</h3>
  <div class="lesson-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Action</th><th>Autonomy level</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Intent classification</td><td>LLM decides (Classifier node)</td><td>Structured output, narrow choice set — Haiku handles this reliably</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>search_docs</code> / <code>search_known_issues</code></td><td>Fully automatic</td><td>Read-only retrieval — no side effect, safe to call freely</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>get_changelog</code></td><td>Fully automatic</td><td>Read-only structured lookup by exact version pair</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>get_ticket_status</code></td><td>Fully automatic</td><td>Read-only; only fetches the authenticated user's own tickets</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>create_support_ticket</code></td><td>Automatic — notify user immediately</td><td>User explicitly requested help; creating a ticket is the expected response when docs fail. No interrupt() needed — but the ticket ID and severity are shown before the conversation ends.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Answering from training knowledge (without retrieval)</td><td><strong>Prohibited</strong></td><td>No citation → no answer. System prompt enforces this absolutely.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Escalation to human queue</td><td>Autonomous when classifier returns 'escalate'</td><td>Data loss and security incidents must reach a human immediately — no LLM deliberation</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <!-- Phase 04 -->
  <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 04 — Design the tools: tool inventory</h3>
  <div class="lesson-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Type</th><th>Reversible?</th><th>Guard</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>search_docs(query, version?)</code></td><td>Read · RAG</td><td>—</td><td>Agent extracts core concept first — never passes the raw question verbatim</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>search_known_issues(query, component?)</code></td><td>Read · RAG</td><td>—</td><td>None — safe to call freely after docs return inconclusive results</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>get_changelog(from, to)</code></td><td>Read · DB</td><td>—</td><td>Exact semver lookup — no embedding needed; user provides version numbers</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>get_ticket_status(ticket_id)</code></td><td>Read · DB</td><td>—</td><td>Enforces ownership: only returns tickets belonging to the authenticated user</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>create_support_ticket(summary, severity, ...)</code></td><td>Write</td><td><strong>No</strong></td><td>Notify user immediately with ticket ID and severity after creation</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>5 tools total — within the 3–5 per agent guideline. All 5 go to the single Resolver agent (Phase 06). No tool is shared across multiple agents in this design, but the MCP server still adds value as shared infrastructure (Phase 04 MCP decision below).</p>

  <LessonCallout variant="tip" title="Applying the data access framework to each knowledge source">
    <p>Three knowledge sources. Different access patterns for each:</p>
    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Knowledge source</th><th>Pattern chosen</th><th>Why not the others</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Product documentation</td>
            <td><strong>RAG Tool</strong> (<code>search_docs</code>)</td>
            <td>Thousands of pages — too large to inject as an MCP Resource on every call. User's question changes every call and is semantic. Keyword SQL search misses paraphrasing.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Known issues</td>
            <td><strong>RAG Tool</strong> (<code>search_known_issues</code>)</td>
            <td>Bug descriptions are written in engineers' language; users describe symptoms in their own words. Only semantic similarity bridges that gap reliably.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Changelog</td>
            <td><strong>DB Tool</strong> (<code>get_changelog</code>)</td>
            <td>User always provides exact version numbers. Exact structured lookup is simpler and faster than semantic retrieval. Semantic search adds no value here.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Ticket status</td>
            <td><strong>DB Tool</strong> (<code>get_ticket_status</code>)</td>
            <td>User provides an exact ticket ID. No semantic matching needed — pure key lookup.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </LessonCallout>

  <LessonCallout variant="warn" title="Why hybrid search for search_docs and search_known_issues">
    <p>
      Pure semantic (vector) search fails on exact technical strings. The error code
      <code>ERR_SCHEMA_CONFLICT</code> is a string, not a concept — it won't embed into a
      meaningful position near "schema validation error" unless those exact words appear in
      the training data. A developer searching by this exact code needs a keyword match, not
      a semantic one.
    </p>
    <p>
      The implementation runs <strong>semantic search and BM25 keyword search in parallel</strong>
      (see code below) then merges results with Reciprocal Rank Fusion. This covers both
      "explain retention policy" (semantic wins) and "ERR_SCHEMA_CONFLICT workaround"
      (keyword wins). Neither alone is sufficient for a technical support domain.
    </p>
  </LessonCallout>

  <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="search-docs-implementation.ts" code={searchDocsCode} />

  <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 04 — Design the tools: tool-to-agent mapping</h3>
  <p>
    One agent in this design (the Resolver) uses all 5 tools. The mapping exercise still matters:
    it confirms there is no accidental sharing and identifies the MCP server boundary.
  </p>
  <div class="lesson-table">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Tool</th><th>Classifier</th><th>Resolver</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>search_docs</code></td><td></td><td>✓</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>search_known_issues</code></td><td></td><td>✓</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>get_changelog</code></td><td></td><td>✓</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>get_ticket_status</code></td><td></td><td>✓</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>create_support_ticket</code></td><td></td><td>✓</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    No tool appears in more than one column within this agent. However, all five tools are
    prime candidates for an MCP server because the HelixDB product has multiple surfaces
    (developer portal, VS Code extension, CLI) that may run their own support agents.
    Implementing the tools once in a shared server means each surface connects and gets
    the same search quality, ticket data, and changelog access.
  </p>

  <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 04 — Design the tools: MCP server decision</h3>
  <p>
    The tools and reference data move into <code>support-tools-server</code>, deployed as an
    HTTP+SSE server at <code>https://support-tools.internal/sse</code>.
    The two documentation corpora appear as MCP Resources — not for runtime retrieval,
    but to feed the offline nightly indexing pipeline that builds the vector stores.
  </p>
  <div class="lesson-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Primitive</th><th>What is exposed</th><th>Why this primitive</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Tools</strong></td>
          <td><code>search_docs</code>, <code>search_known_issues</code>, <code>get_changelog</code>, <code>get_ticket_status</code>, <code>create_support_ticket</code></td>
          <td>Per-call computation — query and version come from the live conversation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Resources</strong></td>
          <td><code>docs://helixdb/all</code>, <code>known-issues://helixdb/active</code></td>
          <td>Static reference corpora refreshed at each release. Consumed by the offline indexing pipeline only — NOT by the agent at runtime. Agent uses Tools.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Prompt Templates</strong></td>
          <td><code>citation-format</code>, <code>ticket-created-reply</code></td>
          <td>Both the support agent and the VS Code extension agent format citations the same way. One template; update wording without redeploying either agent.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Not in this server</strong></td>
          <td><code>get_user_context</code></td>
          <td>User identity and account data live in a separate auth/account service with its own security boundary — not co-mingled with support tools.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="support-tools-server.ts" code={supportMcpServerCode} />

  <!-- Phase 05 -->
  <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 05 — Design state &amp; memory: state schema</h3>
  <p>
    The key design decision: <strong>citations must be state, not just output text.</strong>
    If citations only appear in the generated answer string, they cannot be verified — you can't
    machine-check whether the sentence "Retention policies are set in config.yaml" is actually
    supported by the retrieved chunks without keeping both in state.
  </p>
  <p>
    The schema therefore carries <code>retrievedChunks[]</code> (everything the agent pulled
    from the vector stores this turn) and <code>citations[]</code> (the subset the Resolver
    actually used, mapped to specific claims). A Phase 09 citation verifier node can
    cross-check them before the answer is returned.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="support-state.ts" code={stateCode} />

  <p>
    <code>updatedAt</code> on each chunk is what makes doc-staleness detection possible.
    The Resolver system prompt checks it: if any cited chunk is older than 90 days, the answer
    adds a warning. Without it in state, you can't warn — you just silently serve stale answers.
  </p>

  <!-- Phase 06 -->
  <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 06 — Single agent or multi-agent?</h3>
  <p>
    Apply the three split criteria from the cookbook:
  </p>
  <div class="lesson-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>This system</th><th>Split needed?</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Tool count per agent</td><td>5 tools — within guideline for the Resolver</td><td>No</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Conflicting responsibilities in one system prompt</td><td>"Classify intent" + "retrieve docs + answer" are different cognitive tasks — intent classification is cheap, fast, structured JSON; answering is expensive, generative, citation-heavy</td><td><strong>Yes — separate Classifier</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Specialisation (different models, different tools per context)</td><td>Classifier → Haiku (fast, cheap, no tools). Resolver → Sonnet (generative, 5 tools). Different models for different jobs.</td><td><strong>Yes — two LLM nodes</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Agent?</th><th>Responsibility</th><th>Model / tools</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Context loader</strong></td>
          <td>No — pure function</td>
          <td>Fetches user account (product version, support plan, open tickets). No reasoning needed — simple API call, result written to state.</td>
          <td>No LLM · No tools</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Classifier</strong></td>
          <td>Yes — LLM agent</td>
          <td>Reads question + conversation history. Returns intent as structured JSON: one of documentation / known-issue / version-query / escalate.</td>
          <td>Haiku · No tools</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Resolver</strong></td>
          <td>Yes — LLM agent</td>
          <td>Retrieves from the right knowledge source(s), generates a cited answer, creates a ticket when docs don't answer.</td>
          <td>Sonnet · <code>search_docs</code>, <code>search_known_issues</code>, <code>get_changelog</code>, <code>get_ticket_status</code>, <code>create_support_ticket</code></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>3 nodes total. 2 are LLM agents. Context loader is the exception — it reads account data and writes it to state; no reasoning required.</p>

  <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 06b — Sketch the workflow map</h3>

  <LessonDiagram definition={`
