  <h3 class="lesson-heading">Pattern rationale</h3>
  <div class="lesson-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Pattern</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Context loader → Classifier</strong></td>
          <td>Linear</td>
          <td>Classifier needs user context (product version) to classify correctly. Strict ordering — no parallelism possible here.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Classifier → Resolver / Escalation</strong></td>
          <td>Branching (conditional edge)</td>
          <td><code>state.intent</code> determines the path. Four intents, two destinations (Resolver or Escalation).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Resolver tool calls</strong></td>
          <td>Sequential (LLM-driven)</td>
          <td>Tool calls are ordered by what each returns: docs first, then known issues if inconclusive. The Resolver decides the sequence based on what comes back — not a fixed pipeline.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Parallel inventory-style lookups</strong></td>
          <td>Not applicable here</td>
          <td>Retrieval in this domain is not independent — the result of <code>search_docs</code> determines whether to call <code>search_known_issues</code>. You can't fire them both upfront and then decide.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <!-- Phase 07 -->
  <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 07 — Write the system prompt</h3>
  <p>
    Two LLM-powered nodes, two system prompts. The Classifier prompt is intentionally narrow —
    one output, no tools, no generation. The Resolver prompt carries all the retrieval rules,
    citation instructions, and uncertainty handling. Mixing them would defeat the point of
    separating the nodes.
  </p>

  <h4 class="lesson-heading">Classifier node</h4>
  <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="classifier-prompt.ts" code={classifierPromptCode} />

  <h4 class="lesson-heading">Resolver node</h4>
  <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="resolver-prompt.ts" code={resolverPromptCode} />

  <LessonCallout variant="key" title="Why the citation rule is a hard constraint, not a guideline">
    <p>
      "Try to cite your sources" produces uncited answers when the model is confident.
      The system prompt says <em>every factual claim must be followed by a citation — no exceptions.</em>
      A developer reading an uncited answer has no way to verify it is current, version-correct,
      or not hallucinated. The citation is not polish — it is the trust signal. Without it,
      the agent is no more reliable than the model's training data alone.
    </p>
  </LessonCallout>

  <!-- Phase 08 -->
  <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 08 — Add safety &amp; guardrails: RAG-specific guardrail plan</h3>
  <div class="lesson-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>What it catches</th><th>Applied to</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>L1 — Input validation</strong></td>
          <td>Question not empty, under 4000 chars, user session authenticated</td>
          <td>Every request, before any LLM call or retrieval</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>L2 — Injection detection</strong></td>
          <td>Pattern match for "ignore previous instructions", "forget everything", "you are now". Developer portal is public-facing — adversarial input is expected.</td>
          <td>Every developer question before Classifier</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Citation verifier</strong></td>
          <td>Cross-checks every citation in the Resolver's answer against <code>state.retrievedChunks[]</code>. If a cited source isn't in the retrieved set → flag as a hallucinated citation before returning.</td>
          <td>After Resolver, before returning to user</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Doc staleness warning</strong></td>
          <td>If any cited chunk has <code>updatedAt</code> older than 90 days → append warning to answer. Prevents silently serving outdated docs after a release.</td>
          <td>After Resolver, inline in answer</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Version mismatch warning</strong></td>
          <td>If <code>chunk.docVersion</code> doesn't match <code>state.userContext.productVersion</code> → note the discrepancy in the answer. Never silently serve docs for a version the user isn't running.</td>
          <td>Resolver system prompt + citation verifier</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Ticket deduplication</strong></td>
          <td>Before calling <code>create_support_ticket</code>: check <code>state.userContext.openTickets</code>. If a similar ticket already exists, surface it instead of creating a duplicate.</td>
          <td>Resolver, before ticket creation</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <LessonCallout variant="warn" title="The hardest failure to catch: confident-sounding hallucination">
    <p>
      Hallucinated answers in a technical support context are dangerous because they can cause
      a developer to misconfigure their database, lose data, or waste hours on the wrong fix —
      with high confidence, citing plausible-looking sources.
    </p>
    <p>
      The citation verifier is the primary defence. But the verifier can only catch citations
      to sources that weren't retrieved — it cannot detect when the model paraphrases a retrieved
      chunk incorrectly. That requires a human audit loop (Phase 10).
    </p>
  </LessonCallout>

  <!-- Phase 09 -->
  <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 09 — Error handling &amp; resilience: failure modes</h3>
  <div class="lesson-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Failure</th><th>Response</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Both <code>search_docs</code> and <code>search_known_issues</code> return zero relevant chunks</td>
          <td>Resolver says "I don't have documentation for this." Then calls <code>create_support_ticket()</code>. Never fabricates an answer because the docs are empty.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Retrieved chunks are all older than 90 days</td>
          <td>Answer includes "⚠ These docs were last updated [date] — verify they apply to your release." Does NOT refuse to answer; warns instead.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Chunk doc version doesn't match user's product version</td>
          <td>Answer notes the version mismatch: "This applies to v2.5; you are on v2.3." Does NOT strip the answer; flags the discrepancy.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Citation verifier flags a hallucinated citation</td>
          <td>Strip that claim from the answer before returning. Log the incident for human review. Never return a citation that doesn't trace back to a retrieved chunk.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>create_support_ticket()</code> fails (network error, service down)</td>
          <td>Retry once with exponential backoff. On second failure: tell the user to email support@helixdb.io directly. Never drop the issue silently.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>User asks a question outside HelixDB scope ("what's the best database for X?")</td>
          <td>Respond: "I can only assist with HelixDB Cloud questions." Do not attempt retrieval or redirect to competitor docs.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Classifier returns an intent the routing logic doesn't recognise</td>
          <td>Default to 'documentation' path. Log the unrecognised value as a monitoring event. Never drop the user's question.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <!-- Phase 10 -->
  <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 10 — Observability &amp; testing: metrics</h3>
  <div class="lesson-table">
