  </div>

  <!-- ─── Staff-level design review ───────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 class="lesson-heading">Staff-level design review</h2>
  <p>
    The 10 phases answer <em>how to build</em> this agent. These are the questions a staff
    engineer asks in the design review — after the phases are complete, before sign-off.
    Each is answered for the HelixDB Technical Support Assistant.
  </p>

  <div class="sl-intro">
    <strong>Junior engineer:</strong> "Does this retrieve the right docs?" ·
    <strong>Senior engineer:</strong> "What happens when the docs are wrong or missing?" ·
    <strong>Staff engineer:</strong> "Who owns the knowledge base, and what breaks when it drifts from the product?"
  </div>

  <!-- 1. Problem Validity -->
  <div class="sl-category">
    <div class="sl-cat-header">
      <span class="sl-badge">Problem Validity</span>
      <span class="sl-when">Before Phase 01 — is RAG the right intervention?</span>
    </div>

    <div class="sl-qa">
      <div class="sl-q">What does the non-AI version of this look like, and exactly why is it failing?</div>
      <div class="sl-a">Support engineers manually search the docs site and Confluence for answers, then write replies. It fails for three specific reasons: (1) the same questions are answered from memory every week, producing inconsistent answers — sometimes citing old docs, (2) search tooling on the docs site is keyword-only; developers searching by error code often get zero results, (3) ticket volume grows linearly with the user base while headcount doesn't. We are not replacing a broken process — the process works. We are scaling it without proportional headcount growth.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="sl-qa">
      <div class="sl-q">Why RAG specifically, and not a fine-tuned model or a structured FAQ system?</div>
      <div class="sl-a">Three reasons: (1) the knowledge base changes on every release — fine-tuning would require retraining after every release; RAG reindexes instead, (2) every answer must be traceable — fine-tuned models cannot cite the specific doc version their answer came from; retrieved chunks can, (3) when the answer is wrong, RAG failures are diagnosable (wrong chunk retrieved) — fine-tuning failures are opaque. A structured FAQ only covers anticipated questions; developer support questions are too long-tail to pre-enumerate.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 2. RAG Architecture Decisions -->
  <div class="sl-category">
    <div class="sl-cat-header">
      <span class="sl-badge">RAG Architecture Decisions</span>
      <span class="sl-when">Phase 01 / 04 — who decided on agentic RAG, hybrid search, and why</span>
    </div>

    <div class="sl-qa">
      <div class="sl-q">Why agentic RAG instead of a pipeline (always retrieve first, then generate)?</div>
      <div class="sl-a">Three retrieval paths exist: docs search (vector), known-issues search (vector), and changelog lookup (structured). A pipeline that always fires all three wastes latency and tokens — a version-query never needs <code>search_known_issues</code>, and a known-issue query shouldn't run <code>get_changelog</code>. The Classifier routes to the right starting point, and the Resolver adapts: if <code>search_docs</code> returns inconclusive results, it calls <code>search_known_issues</code> next. A fixed pipeline can't make that decision. The conditionality is what justifies the agent overhead.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="sl-qa">
      <div class="sl-q">Who decided on hybrid search, and was it tested against semantic-only?</div>
      <div class="sl-a">The decision was triggered by a specific failure: during prototyping, <code>ERR_SCHEMA_CONFLICT</code> typed verbatim by a user returned zero results from pure vector search because the embedding for the error code string landed far from the semantic cluster for "schema validation error." Hybrid search (BM25 + vector, merged with Reciprocal Rank Fusion) fixed that class of failure. We ran a golden-set evaluation: 200 known Q&amp;A pairs. Semantic-only: 78% hit rate. Hybrid: 94%. The 16-point gain justified the implementation complexity. This is documented in ADR-0051.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="sl-qa">
      <div class="sl-q">What is the chunking strategy, and who decided it?</div>
      <div class="sl-a">Section-level chunking by heading: each chunk is one section of a documentation page, bounded by H2/H3 headings. The heading is stored as metadata and used as the citation's section field. We evaluated paragraph-level and page-level alternatives: paragraph-level improved precision but hurt recall on questions spanning multiple paragraphs; page-level hurt precision because similarity scores get diluted across topics. Section-level was the best trade-off. One open question: code blocks. Currently embedded as part of the section. If retrieval quality on code-heavy questions degrades, we will evaluate storing code blocks as separate chunks with a "code" type tag. This is tracked in ADR-0052.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 3. Failure Imagination -->
  <div class="sl-category">
    <div class="sl-cat-header">
      <span class="sl-badge">Failure Imagination</span>
      <span class="sl-when">Phases 08–09 — RAG-specific failure modes at P99</span>
    </div>

    <div class="sl-qa">
      <div class="sl-q">What's the worst plausible failure mode we haven't explicitly defended against?</div>
      <div class="sl-a">A doc page that is factually wrong — not missing, wrong. The engineering team publishes an incorrect workaround for a known issue. Our agent retrieves it, cites it correctly, and confidently gives developers bad advice. The citation verifier won't catch it — the citation is real, the source is real, only the content is wrong. This is a documentation quality problem, not an agent problem. The defence is outside the agent: a doc review process for known-issue workarounds before publication, and a feedback loop (thumbs down on answers) that routes to the doc owner when the same source gets repeated negative ratings. We cannot solve upstream content quality inside the agent.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="sl-qa">
      <div class="sl-q">What happens when the product ships a breaking change and the docs aren't updated yet?</div>
      <div class="sl-a">The agent serves outdated docs for the gap period between release and doc update. The 90-day staleness warning doesn't fire because the docs are recent — they're just wrong for the new version. The only defence is process: a mandatory doc-update checklist on every release PR, enforced in CI. The agent can show "⚠ Last updated [date]" but cannot distinguish "old but still correct" from "old and now wrong." This is the most dangerous failure mode for a product that releases frequently, and it is a team process problem, not a retrieval problem.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="sl-qa">
      <div class="sl-q">Can a developer manipulate the agent into revealing another customer's ticket data?</div>
      <div class="sl-a"><code>get_ticket_status(ticket_id)</code> at the tool layer enforces ownership: it validates that the ticket belongs to the authenticated user before returning data. The agent trusting its own state (that the user is who they say they are) is not enough — the tool validates independently. If a developer guesses another customer's ticket ID and asks for its status, the tool returns a 403. The agent cannot be prompted into bypassing this because the check is at the service layer, not in the LLM. This is defence in depth: agent cannot override what the service refuses to return.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 4. Knowledge Base Operations -->
  <div class="sl-category">
    <div class="sl-cat-header">
      <span class="sl-badge">Knowledge Base Operations</span>
      <span class="sl-when">Phase 10 — who owns the KB and what happens when it drifts</span>
    </div>

    <div class="sl-qa">
      <div class="sl-q">Who owns the knowledge base, and what is their obligation when the product changes?</div>
      <div class="sl-a">Named owner: Technical Writing team lead, with a named backup per product area (ingestion, query engine, SDK). Their obligation is explicit in the team charter: every release must include a doc PR before the product PR merges. The agent team's obligation is equally explicit: the nightly indexing pipeline must complete successfully and emit a metric (KB freshness: minutes since last successful index). If that metric goes stale for more than 24 hours, the agent team is paged — not the docs team. Keeping the KB current is a people process. Detecting when the pipeline fails to refresh it is the agent team's responsibility.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="sl-qa">
      <div class="sl-q">What does the escalation rate tell you about the knowledge base?</div>
      <div class="sl-a">Escalation rate ≥ 30% is the target ceiling, not a success metric. It is a proxy for KB gaps: if 30% of questions can't be answered from docs, 30% of your KB is missing. We track escalation by question category — if version-query escalations spike, the changelog coverage is incomplete. If known-issue escalations spike, the known-issues index is behind. This dashboard is reviewed weekly by the docs team lead, not just the engineering team. The agent is generating a continuous content gap report as a side effect of running. Use it.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 5. Hallucination Prevention -->
  <div class="sl-category">
    <div class="sl-cat-header">
      <span class="sl-badge">Hallucination Prevention</span>
      <span class="sl-when">Phases 07–08 — why "grounded only" is harder than it sounds</span>
    </div>

    <div class="sl-qa">
      <div class="sl-q">The system prompt says "never answer from training knowledge." How do you know when it's violated?</div>
      <div class="sl-a">The citation verifier catches one class of violation: citations to sources not in the retrieved set. But it cannot catch the harder case: the model paraphrases a retrieved chunk and adds one uncited sentence from training knowledge. This requires LLM-as-judge evaluation: a grounding evaluator model reads the answer and the retrieved chunks and scores whether every sentence in the answer is supported by at least one chunk. We run this on 10% of interactions via LangSmith sampling. If grounding score drops below 95%, we halt and investigate. Without this sampling loop, the instruction "never answer from training" is unenforceable.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="sl-qa">
      <div class="sl-q">What happens when a developer asks a question the model knows the answer to — correctly — but it isn't in the docs?</div>
      <div class="sl-a">The agent says "I don't have documentation on this" and creates a ticket. Even if the model's training data contains the correct answer. This is a deliberate design choice, not an oversight. The reason: the agent's value proposition is traceable answers from an authoritative, versioned knowledge source. If we allow the model to answer from training knowledge when it's "pretty sure," we lose the citation guarantee entirely. The trade-off is that some developers get a slower resolution than necessary. The correct fix is to add that topic to the knowledge base — the ticket created by the agent is exactly how that gap gets discovered and filled.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 6. Economics -->
  <div class="sl-category">
    <div class="sl-cat-header">
      <span class="sl-badge">Economics at Scale</span>
      <span class="sl-when">Phase 10 — retrieval cost curves and token budgets</span>
    </div>

    <div class="sl-qa">
      <div class="sl-q">What does this cost at 10× current volume, and are there non-linear cost risks?</div>
      <div class="sl-a">Current: 300 interactions/day. Classifier (Haiku): ~200 tokens each = $0.001/interaction. Resolver (Sonnet): ~3000 tokens input (question + 5 retrieved chunks) + ~500 tokens output = ~$0.02/interaction. Total: ~$0.021/interaction. At 10×: $0.021 × 3000 = $63/day, $23K/year. Against the cost of 2 additional support engineers at $150K/year each, this math is easy. The non-linear risk is vector search at scale: pgvector with ANN indexes stays fast to ~50M vectors; beyond that, latency grows and we may need a managed vector DB. We've capacity-planned the index at 100× before needing to upgrade. No cost cliff in the current architecture.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="sl-qa">
      <div class="sl-q">What is the cost of a hallucination incident at scale?</div>
      <div class="sl-a">A misconfiguration caused by a hallucinated answer: 1–4 hours of developer debugging time × developer hourly rate + support call to resolve + reputational cost. For a paid enterprise customer, a hallucinated answer that causes data loss is a potential SLA violation and churn risk. Prevention cost: the grounding evaluator runs on 10% of interactions at ~$0.005 per evaluation = $0.0005 per interaction added. At current volume: $0.15/day. Prevention is 25× cheaper than a single incident. This is not a close call — the grounding evaluator is mandatory before launch.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 7. Platform Thinking -->
  <div class="sl-category">
    <div class="sl-cat-header">
      <span class="sl-badge">Platform Thinking</span>
      <span class="sl-when">Phase 06 — is this the first of one or the first of ten?</span>
    </div>

    <div class="sl-qa">
      <div class="sl-q">Is the support-tools-server and the knowledge base specific to this agent or shared infrastructure?</div>
      <div class="sl-a">Shared infrastructure from day one. The VS Code extension has a "help" panel that asks the same questions in a different UI. The CLI has a <code>helix help</code> command that should give the same answers. If each is its own agent with its own copy of the retrieval tools, we have three implementations of hybrid search, three citation formats, three KB indexes that drift independently. The <code>support-tools-server</code> is the single implementation that all three connect to via MCP. The vector index is rebuilt by one nightly pipeline. The citation Prompt Template is maintained in one place. This decision must be made before the second surface ships — retrofitting shared infrastructure after the fact is expensive.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="sl-qa">
      <div class="sl-q">What must exist before the agent team can hand this off?</div>
      <div class="sl-a">Five artifacts: (1) ADRs 0051–0055 covering hybrid search decision, chunking strategy, citation verifier design, KB ownership policy, and cost model, (2) the golden Q&amp;A dataset — 200 curated pairs covering all four intent categories, runnable as a regression suite, (3) the grounding evaluator, wired into CI, configured to block merges when grounding score drops below 95%, (4) the KB ownership doc — named owners per product area, doc-update SLAs, escalation chain when the pipeline fails, (5) the runbook: one page per alert type, tested in a gameday before handoff. Without these, the next team can operate the agent but cannot safely evolve it.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 8. Knowledge Gaps -->
  <div class="sl-category">
    <div class="sl-cat-header">
      <span class="sl-badge">Knowledge Gaps as a Product Signal</span>
      <span class="sl-when">Phase 10 — what tickets teach you</span>
    </div>

    <div class="sl-qa">
      <div class="sl-q">How does the agent create value beyond answering individual questions?</div>
      <div class="sl-a">Every <code>create_support_ticket</code> call is a data point: a question that the KB couldn't answer. Aggregate those tickets by topic over 30 days and you have a ranked list of documentation gaps — ordered by developer demand. The docs team has always had to guess which topics need more coverage. The agent makes that list automatic and continuous. A weekly report from the ticket system to the docs team lead, grouped by category and sorted by frequency, is more valuable than any docs analytics tool. The agent is not just a support tool — it is a continuous KB gap detector. This feedback loop must be built before launch, not added later.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="sl-qa">
      <div class="sl-q">What's the difference between a KB gap and a product UX problem?</div>
      <div class="sl-a">If the same question appears in tickets 50 times in a month, it might mean: (1) the docs don't cover it — a KB gap, (2) the docs cover it but are hard to find or unclear — a docs UX problem, (3) the product behaviour itself is confusing enough that even clear docs don't prevent the question — a product UX problem. The agent can't distinguish these three. A human needs to read the ticket clusters monthly and make that call. Routing every ticket to the docs team without this triage will cause them to document things that should be fixed in the product instead. Named owner for this monthly triage: the product manager for developer experience.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <LessonMindMap content={mindMap} />

</LessonLayout>

